Make CONFIG_ANDROID_BINDER_IPC_32BIT settable on a 32-bit kernel.

Upstream declares the symbol as a bare `bool` with no prompt string, so it is
not user-configurable: kconfig discards whatever a defconfig or fragment says
about it and recomputes it from `default y`. On a 32-bit kernel at 4.17 or
older that makes the legacy binder wire protocol (version 7) unavoidable by
configuration, even though the driver supports protocol 8 perfectly well and
every 32-bit userspace built for protocol 8 needs it.

Adding a prompt makes the symbol settable in both directions. `default y` is
kept, so a kernel that says nothing about it behaves exactly as before and no
existing configuration changes meaning. A platform moving to protocol 8 adds
`# CONFIG_ANDROID_BINDER_IPC_32BIT is not set` to its defconfig, which now has
an effect.

Applies to 4.9 through 4.17. The symbol was removed upstream from 4.18, where
protocol 8 is the only option and no patch is needed.

--- a/drivers/android/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/android/Kconfig
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@ config ANDROID_BINDER_IPC
 	  between said processes.
 
 config ANDROID_BINDER_IPC_32BIT
-	bool
+	bool "Use the legacy 32-bit binder wire protocol (version 7)"
 	depends on !64BIT && ANDROID_BINDER_IPC
 	default y
 	---help---
